# Building and releasing

This project uses `main` for integration and annotated Git tags for versions.
Hatchling and `uv-dynamic-versioning` derive the package version from Git;
do not manually edit a version field or use a version override for releases.
Run `uv lock` and commit `uv.lock` before using the release workflow.

## Development and validation

Add Towncrier fragments under `changelog.d/` for user-visible changes using
the categories configured in `pyproject.toml`.

```console
make test
make lint
make typecheck
make build
make check-dist
make check
```

`make test` runs pytest on the active Python. `make check` runs lint before
tests, type checking{% if docs %}, a strict documentation build{% endif %},
builds the sdist and wheel, and validates both. `make check-dist` requires
exactly one matching wheel and sdist in `dist/`; it checks their metadata and
contents with Twine, rebuilds the wheel from the sdist, and installs both
wheels in isolated environments, importing the package and loading its console
entry points. Old files in `dist/` must be removed
explicitly before checking another version. `TAG=v1.2.3` additionally requires
a clean checkout, an annotated tag at the current commit, and an exact version
match.
{% if tasks == "nox" %}
`make nox` runs the supported-Python Nox matrix. For a focused run, pass a
test path after `--`, for example
`uv run noxfile.py -s tests-3.13 -- tests/test_version.py`.
{% endif -%}
{% if docs %}
## Documentation

Use `make docs` for a clean, strict Zensical build and `make docs-serve` for a
local preview. CI validates documentation on pull requests and publishes the
validated `main` commit to GitHub Pages after checks succeed. A successful
final PyPI release also deploys the tagged documentation. TestPyPI releases
do not deploy it. Coverage data from the same validation run produces
`coverage/` and `coverage.svg` on the site.

In repository settings, choose GitHub Actions as the Pages source and allow
the `github-pages` environment to deploy from `main`.
{% endif %}
## Prepare a release

Use canonical `X.Y.Z` versions, optionally followed by `aN`, `bN`, or `rcN`.
Start on clean `main` containing `origin/main`, with a committed lockfile.
Versions must advance existing local and remote tags.

```console
make release-notes VERSION=1.2.0
uv run --group release python -m scripts.release --dry-run 1.2.0
make release VERSION=1.2.0
```

Preparation runs in an isolated clone and validates the tagged artifacts.
The dry run leaves the caller's files and refs unchanged. A final release
consumes pending fragments into the changelog; a prerelease keeps them.
Successful preparation advances local `main` and creates an annotated tag,
but does not push, publish, or create a GitHub release. The command prints
the explicit atomic push command. Pushing the tag only validates it.

## Publish explicitly

After pushing the prepared commit and tag, run:

```console
make publish TAG=v1.2.0
```

The command requires the GitHub CLI, dispatches the release workflow, and
watches that specific run. Prereleases go to TestPyPI; final releases go to
PyPI. CI builds and checks fresh artifacts from the tagged commit and uploads
those exact files. It creates a GitHub release only after publication succeeds.

Configure Trusted Publishing on PyPI and TestPyPI for repository
`{{ repository_path }}`, workflow `release.yaml`, and the corresponding
`pypi` and `testpypi` GitHub environments. Configure each index separately;
the workflow uses OIDC and does not require long-lived API tokens.

If preparation fails, inspect the retained checkout path printed by the tool.
If a dispatch times out or publication partially succeeds, inspect the named
Actions run and package index before retrying. Do not move or recreate tags or
blindly upload a different artifact under an existing version.
